1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:03,850
A post comes down onto a sill.

2
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,740
A tongue on its end slides
into a hole, and a square pin

3
00:00:09,780 --> 00:00:13,890
is driven across, pulling
the two tight together.

4
00:00:14,233 --> 00:00:16,783
This is the mortise and tenon:

5
00:00:17,173 --> 00:00:20,366
in Japanese, hozo and hozo-ana.

6
00:00:26,290 --> 00:00:28,890
Hozo is the tenon, the tongue.

7
00:00:29,215 --> 00:00:34,270
Hozo-ana, literally tenon
hole, is the mortise.

8
00:00:34,940 --> 00:00:37,910
Dictionaries note an older reading, hoso.

9
00:00:38,333 --> 00:00:40,523
There are many named kinds.

10
00:00:41,013 --> 00:00:47,093
A stepped tenon, the jūhozo, or
stacked tenon, for example, goes at

11
00:00:47,133 --> 00:00:51,423
the top of a post where a wall
plate and a beam stack up.

12
00:00:52,553 --> 00:00:56,723
This episode looks at the joint
where a post meets a sill.

13
00:00:59,833 --> 00:01:04,073
For posts outside the
bearing walls, the Flat 35

14
00:01:04,113 --> 00:01:08,423
house specification lists
two families of options.

15
00:01:08,953 --> 00:01:13,963
A short tenon, held with a
metal plate, bracket or staple.

16
00:01:14,653 --> 00:01:18,177
Or a long tenon, pinned with a komisen.

17
00:01:18,767 --> 00:01:24,287
At an outside corner, the corner
post can take the ōgi-hozo, a

18
00:01:24,327 --> 00:01:28,657
fan tenon with a trapezoid
section, again with hardware.

19
00:01:32,300 --> 00:01:34,650
How big is a short tenon?

20
00:01:35,260 --> 00:01:39,940
The test standard for Japan's
certified joint hardware uses

21
00:01:39,980 --> 00:01:43,770
one on a hundred-and-five
millimeter post and sill:

22
00:01:43,835 --> 00:01:48,410
thirty thick, eighty-five
wide, fifty deep.

23
00:01:48,800 --> 00:01:52,960
In a bearing wall, Japan's
standard detail drawings

24
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:56,910
give a plain short tenon
a joint rating of zero:

25
00:01:57,720 --> 00:02:01,170
it isn't counted on to
stop the post pulling out.

26
00:02:01,800 --> 00:02:06,610
That job needs rated hardware,
or a pinned long tenon.

27
00:02:10,667 --> 00:02:13,837
The long tenon goes deep into the sill.

28
00:02:14,447 --> 00:02:21,197
A hardwood pin, the komisen, is driven
sideways through sill and tenon together.

29
00:02:22,247 --> 00:02:26,187
Where a tenon passes right
through and sticks out, it

30
00:02:26,227 --> 00:02:29,577
can instead be pinned
through that projecting part:

31
00:02:30,027 --> 00:02:31,417
a hanasen.

32
00:02:31,733 --> 00:02:36,243
The holes are made so the pin
draws the two members together:

33
00:02:37,013 --> 00:02:40,293
the hole through the tenon
is bored slightly closer

34
00:02:40,333 --> 00:02:43,083
to the shoulder than the
hole through the sill.

35
00:02:44,233 --> 00:02:48,523
Driving the pin pulls the
post down tight onto the sill.

36
00:02:48,833 --> 00:02:53,863
The public-works specification
asks for square holes and pins;

37
00:02:54,633 --> 00:02:58,563
one lab report notes round
pins are now more common.

38
00:02:59,813 --> 00:03:05,313
In a bearing wall, the Flat 35
specification puts a pinned long

39
00:03:05,353 --> 00:03:09,783
tenon in the same class as an
L-shaped metal corner plate.

40
00:03:13,900 --> 00:03:15,610
Cutting the tenon:

41
00:03:16,020 --> 00:03:19,580
rip down the cheeks
first, then crosscut the

42
00:03:19,620 --> 00:03:23,130
shoulder, with the kerf on
the waste side of its line.

43
00:03:24,060 --> 00:03:26,120
The public-works specification warns:

44
00:03:26,160 --> 00:03:29,310
don't saw the crosscuts too deep.

45
00:03:29,633 --> 00:03:35,373
In the skills-test manual's practice
piece, a beam's tenon is cut a little

46
00:03:35,413 --> 00:03:41,229
loose in thickness and a little tight
in width, as we saw in Episode 4.

47
00:03:41,933 --> 00:03:43,603
Chopping the mortise:

48
00:03:44,013 --> 00:03:48,083
use a mortise chisel a little
narrower than the hole.

49
00:03:48,633 --> 00:03:53,183
Start a few millimeters inside
the lines, rough it out,

50
00:03:53,833 --> 00:03:57,123
then pare back to the
lines and check the depth.

51
00:03:57,567 --> 00:03:59,117
The manual adds:

52
00:03:59,467 --> 00:04:02,727
strike lightly when the
edge runs along the grain,

53
00:04:02,767 --> 00:04:06,397
and a little harder
when it cuts across it.

54
00:04:09,467 --> 00:04:11,597
Fit it in small steps.

55
00:04:12,367 --> 00:04:15,927
In the manual's practice
piece, the width goes in with

56
00:04:15,967 --> 00:04:20,817
light taps over a block of
wood; the thickness, by hand.

57
00:04:21,787 --> 00:04:26,577
For posts, the public-works
specification asks for a snug fit:

58
00:04:26,747 --> 00:04:28,597
driven in, and fully seated.

59
00:04:31,300 --> 00:04:32,060
Next time:

60
00:04:32,100 --> 00:04:35,530
where one beam crosses over another.

61
00:04:36,567 --> 00:04:39,237
A short tenon needs hardware to hold.

62
00:04:40,007 --> 00:04:44,237
A long tenon can be pinned
so it pulls itself tight.

63
00:04:45,067 --> 00:04:49,477
And a snug fit, made in
small steps, does the rest.

64
00:04:59,651 --> 00:05:00,853
One note:

65
00:05:00,921 --> 00:05:06,013
sharp-tool technique, and how
to use these tools safely, is

66
00:05:06,053 --> 00:05:09,683
best learned from a teacher,
at a bench, in person.
